WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.460
Bash scripts rarely operate on values that are entirely internal.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:20.600
They receive command-line arguments, environment variables, configuration files, standard input, filenames, directory contents, job parameters, CI/CD metadata, API responses, and output from other commands.

3
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.420
Any of these values can influence what the script does.

4
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:26.580
Some inputs are obvious because a person typed them.

5
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.040
Others are indirect.

6
00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.500
A branch name may become part of a build workflow.

7
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.120
A generated file may become an argument to a packaging step.

8
00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.780
A value returned by a service may decide which environment is processed.

9
00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.800
A command output line may become the next command's input.

10
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:48.460
Defensive scripting treats these as trust boundaries, even when the source is familiar.

11
00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.620
Internal data still deserves validation.

12
00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:59.960
A value can be missing, stale, truncated, malformed, unexpectedly empty, or shaped differently after a tool version changes.

13
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:08.700
A script may run in a different scheduler, shell environment, container image, workspace, or CI runner than the author originally expected.

14
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:19.060
Risk increases when input can affect command selection, option selection, path selection, redirection, working directory, or the data passed to an external tool.

15
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:23.400
The more authority the command has, the more carefully that boundary should be defined.

16
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:31.180
A backup script, release script, or administrative script deserves stricter input handling than a local one-off helper.

17
00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:34.180
The first prevention step is inventory.

18
00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:44.040
Ask where values enter the script, which values can influence execution, and which values cross from less trusted context into more privileged automation.

19
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:51.900
That exercise is useful even when there is no attacker in mind because it finds reliability problems and review gaps too.

20
00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:56.260
Once inputs are identified, the script can define what is allowed.

21
00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:09.320
Arguments can be constrained, environment assumptions can be documented, config values can be checked, filenames can be handled as data, and service responses can be validated before they affect command execution.

22
00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.460
Prevention begins before the command line is assembled.
